Richard Deutsch joining us from Toronto  the Sports Illustrated media critic  columnist and Richard thanks for joining  us give me be philosophy about doing it  this way with TNT TBS as opposed to the  last 130 years on CBS dan good to be  with you I feel like I should be calling  you from Smyrna you know Turner has had  the option to do the selection show  based on the contract they signed with  CBS they opted in the past not to do it  they decided to take that option this  year with the philosophy that like you  said people will find it the difference  between something on cable and something  on television on network it's not so  great with the exception of viewership  where it's great and I think Turner just  wants to put its own stamp on this  process that's why you're seeing you  know a 300 person audience come in and  that's why you're seeing as everyone has  talked about these changes where the  plan is to unveil in the first 10  minutes in alphabetical order all the  teams then the matchups are slotted into  the seeds and the brackets the teams  will be grouped by auto bed and at-large  and according to CBS and Turner  they say all of this including and the  unveiling in the brackets will be done  within 45 minutes where I think they're  making a little bit too much of a  mistake is that I understand TBS wants  to put its own you know it's sort of its  own spin on stuff and you can understand  that it's the first time doing this  they're excited they have a lot of smart  people there but and I'm stealing alive  from Dan Walken and I think you've said  this on your show earlier the brackets  are the star of the show I mean it's not  changing this up to sort of come up with  some kind of new unique format to give  us all the teams that are going to be in  the tournament that information in the  end is really kind of irrelevant without  the bracket part of the show so if I was  an executive I would have done with CBS  did last year they listen to their  viewers they decided not to extend this  for an hour plus they got the brackets  out fast and then basically you know  stay with your conviction don't worry  that ESPN is going to be doing this on  their airway  and try to keep people tuned to your  network because you offer really  interesting analysis yeah I agree with  that philosophy here and I understand  that they want to make it theirs and  there are certain things that they're  going to try that in five years they may  not be doing it but I do want to sell  our analysis here our experts here and I  wouldn't be worried about ESPN and I I  would just treat this as how do we do  the best possible show instead of saying  oh my gosh they're gonna all leave us  you know after 32 minutes here and I  think sometimes you can out think  yourself when it comes to these these  shows I think you're absolutely this is  why you should be running Network Stan  you're absolutely right in this case in  this case you're correct I think they're  doing a little bit too much because they  want to separate themselves from CBS and  here's actually the reason and you  mention this at the top a little bit I  have some numbers here that'll sort of  back this up the fact is because it's on  TBS this year the viewership is gonna be  the lowest of all time that's just a  product of network versus cable TBS  having 23 million less homes than CBS I  look at the viewership over the last  token back nine years now  in 2009 CBS s election show drew seven  million viewers big number last year  dropped to four point eight million so  the audience is leaving a lot of that  has to do with of course changing  viewership and people migrating from  cable to just ordinarily world and  everything else so you make the  presumption if I'm TBS that you're gonna  lose viewers anyway just because of the  changing shape of television why make  such a dramatic change why not give the  people at least who have stayed loyal to  this election show what they want and  it's overwhelmingly clear what they want  and they want brackets quickly they want  to see the stuff on the screen so they  could start getting in their minds who  they're gonna pick for their office  pools and that's where I think some of  this blowback  I think Turner like you said sort of  out-thought itself a little bit there  there's no need to change you know the  wheel this dramatic you could put some  nice touches on the wheel but you know  you don't have to take a motorcycle  where you were driving a Ford  for a while Richard Dyke Sports  Illustrated media writer let me get a  Peyton Manning question in there for you  Richard before I let you go is he in  play for an analyst job does he does he  consider himself in play first of all I  think ESPN Dan would give Tate in the  building perhaps they'd even give him  the new presidency if he would take this  job I think they really would like I  think he's more in play this year and  then he ever has before he he has a very  big agent the Montague group that's  doing his broadcasting I'm sure Dan  you've heard of that so it's a little  inside baseball but sandy Montague the  guy is represented very famous people  from Mike Tirico to Bob Costas et cetera  and so I think you know I think Satan is  from what I understand I think he's  considering the possibility of  broadcasting more than he ever has  before but from everything I understand  he hasn't given any indication that he's  definitely going to do it I think if he  did decide to do it this year  I think the Fox job would be far more  appealing to him than the Monday Night  Football job giving travel giving it's  on a network and given that Fox I think  would be very very good in terms of  giving Payton whatever he wanted  regarding schedule I mean Payton  probably could leave that game I would  imagine Fox so you know they might even  have a [ __ ] plane for him Dan literally  outside of the stadium and flying back  wherever he needs to be so if he ends up  doing it I think Fox would be the only  place he would do it this year but I  think I would still bet the under he's  always and you've had you've interviewed  him anytime  he's always spoken about something  larger than broadcasting like ownership  or management so I'd be surprised if he  actually do you think Tony Romo and his  impact had any or has any impact on  Payton's future doing this I do I yeah  because I think I think Payton who's a  very bright guy saw Tony have such  immense success both critically and with  viewers in his first year and Tony was  really a product of preparation when it  came to broadcasting and CBS say their  credit had a great plan they basically  did a camp with Tony where months before  he was ever going to go on air CBS  people including Jim Nantz came down to  Dallas  Tony essentially had a broadcasting  trainer which was pretty fast  to work with em on just the mechanics of  the profession so yeah I think Peyton  saw that and if nothing else that you  know what there's a guy who did not have  any broadcasting reps like me who came  in he's arguably either the best or the  second best NFL analyst working today  depending on how you feel about Cris  Collinsworth so I think that probably  did give Peyton at least a little bit of  maybe some rethought in terms of you  know I could come in and do this at a  high level the way Tony did I'm a big  Tony Romo fan book I think he was  tremendous and I appreciate people who  take analysis to the second level just  to sort of teach me things about the  game and I thought he was a revelation  this year Richard thank you for the  insights we appreciate it safe travels  back from Toronto thanks that's Richard  Deutsch sports illustrated media critic  for more Dan Patrick show to the  audience Channel 239 on DirecTV or  download the Dan Patrick show app  [Music]

